; electronics: the engineering of signal processing and control using
; semiconductor devices. operates at low power — information, not energy.
; 
; the semiconductor revolution:
; 1947: transistor (Bardeen, Brattain, Shockley at Bell Labs).
; 1958: integrated circuit (Kilby, Noyce).
; Moore's law: transistor count doubles every ~2 years.
; 2023: ~50 billion transistors on a single chip (Apple M2 Ultra).
; 
; fundamental devices:
; diode:       current flows one way only (p-n junction).
; rectification (AC → DC), protection, LEDs, solar cells.
; transistor:  current-controlled switch or amplifier.
; BJT: base current controls collector current. hFE = Ic/Ib.
; MOSFET: gate voltage controls drain current. fundamental digital switch.
; op-amp:      ideal voltage amplifier. inverting, non-inverting, summing, diff.
; virtual ground, negative feedback — the grammar of analog design.
; 
; analog vs digital:
; analog:  continuous voltage signals. audio amplifiers, sensors, RF circuits.
; noise accumulates. bandwidth limited.
; digital: discrete voltage levels (0 and 1). logic gates. noise immune.
; can be copied perfectly. the basis of all computation.
; 
; the analog-digital boundary:
; ADC (analog-to-digital converter): sample + quantise. Nyquist: fs > 2fmax.
; DAC (digital-to-analog converter): reconstruct signal from digital values.
; this boundary is where signal processing lives.
; 
; PCB (printed circuit board): the physical substrate of electronic systems.
; copper traces on fibreglass (FR4) substrate.
; layers: 2, 4, 6, 8... layers for complex routing.
; SMD (surface mount) vs through-hole components.
; physics connections
; key concepts

